            <ArticleTitle>Tracheobronchopathy From Inhaled Corticosteroids.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0012-3692(17)31429-0</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.chest.2017.08.013</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Inhaled corticosteroids (ICSs) have become the mainstay of asthma control. They are also recommended as an add-on therapy to long-acting beta agonists and anticholinergics in moderate to severe COPD with recurrent exacerbations. Ultimately this clinical practice has led to the widespread use of ICSs, which are supported by a more favorable side effect profile than that of systemic steroids.</AbstractText>
